chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 24 of the 3rd edition, Text-to-Speech and Speech Recognition Engines, teaches pyttsx3 (the engine, say, runAndWait, the voice, rate and volume properties and save_to_file for WAV files), Whisper (load_model, transcribe and Table 24-1 of model sizes and runtimes), subtitle files (the writers of whisper.utils) and yt-dlp (download and extract_info), and ends with 7 practice questions and 3 practice programs [P01]. The page was fetched on 2026-10-14 and returned 200 with 44,667 bytes, holds 17 listings and 1 table, and its sha256 equals the chapterDigest the book's own chapter ontology records [P01] [P03] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The online materials"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The online materials of the 3rd edition (7,759,877 bytes, 91 files, 72 of them Python) hold hello_tts.py as the only file of this chapter and no file that imports whisper or yt_dlp, and none of hello.wav, audio.srt or metadata.json; the publisher's book page (47,616 bytes) has 1 occurrence of the chapter title and 0 of Whisper, pyttsx3 and yt-dlp [P01] [P02] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P04 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The pins of Appendix A"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Appendix A pins pyttsx3 == 2.98 and openai-whisper==20231117, advises numpy<2 and the CUDA 11.8 wheel index of PyTorch, says the automateboringstuff3 package always installs the newest yt-dlp, and names neither ffmpeg nor espeak [P05]. PyPI lists 22 releases of pyttsx3 with 2.99 (2025-07-08) as the newest, 13 of openai-whisper with 20250625 (2025-06-26) as the newest and 641 of yt-dlp with 2026.8.19 as the newest stable one, which needs Python 3.10 or later [P16] [P28] [P40]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P05, chx:P16, chx:P28, chx:P40 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The pinned Whisper does not install"@en ; sen0414:findingText "pip download of openai-whisper==20231117, whose only file on PyPI is a source archive, ended with return code 1 and ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pkg_resources' on Python 3.12.3 with pip 26.2.1, and the same release installed after setuptools below 81 was installed and the build isolation was switched off; of the 13 releases, 6 (20230307 to 20231106) could not be installed on Python 3.12 at all because they require triton 2.0.0, which has no wheel for it [P28]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P28 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The pinned pyttsx3 saves a truncated file"@en ; sen0414:findingText "With pyttsx3 2.98, the release Appendix A pins, the sentence 'Hello. How are you doing?' was saved as 0.6 s of audio where 2.99 saved 1.54 s, and 'One. Two. Three.' as 0.51 s against 1.55 s; the 2.98 driver writes the file at the first end-of-sentence event before it adds the samples of that event, and it hands the file to ffmpeg, so a name ending in .mp3 gave a real MP3 file, .ogg a real Ogg file, and with no ffmpeg on the PATH no file at all, while 2.99 writes a WAV through the wave module whatever the extension says [P05] [P16] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P05, chx:P16, chx:P19 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The package the chapter names"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's Linux command is sudo apt install espeak. On Ubuntu 24.04 pyttsx3 2.99 raised RuntimeError (This means you probably do not have eSpeak or eSpeak-ng installed) with neither package, raised ValueError (SetVoiceByName failed with unknown return code -1 for voice: gmw/en) with only espeak 1.48.15, and started with only espeak-ng 1.51 (131 voices, a saved WAV, the library libespeak-ng.so.1) and with both [P01] [P19] [P22] [P24]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P19, chx:P22, chx:P24 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "say only queues"@en ; sen0414:findingText "say() returned None at once, no event had fired and isBusy() already reported True; a setProperty was not applied before a runAndWait that had something to say (the rate read 200 after setProperty('rate', 300) and 300 after the call); say('') returned the text Argument value can't be None or empty instead of raising, save_to_file('') raised AssertionError, and with no sound card (/dev/snd absent, aplay installed) runAndWait returned with exit code 0 although aplay printed an audio open error [P01] [P18] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P18, chx:P19 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The loop can hang"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A runAndWait with nothing to say after a property change never returned when it was the second call, and save_to_file into a folder that does not exist never returned after a first utterance (both killed after 15 s), while the same call as the first of a process returned; in 12 fresh processes of the recorded run a 33-word text was not yet a complete file when the first runAndWait returned in 8 and a 5-word text in none, so a program must wait for the file [P18] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P18, chx:P19 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The voices"@en ; sen0414:findingText "eSpeak NG 1.51 gave 131 Voice objects through pyttsx3 2.99, the first being Afrikaans with the gender Male, the age None and the languages ['af']; all 131 said Male, none had an age, 8 were English, the voice the engine starts with is gmw/en, and no name contains David or Zira, the two Windows voices the chapter prints; the chapter's listing 4 then sets the voice to voices[1].id, which is sem/am (Amharic), and the same engine makes the hello.wav of listing 5, which lasted 0.89 s instead of 1.54 s [P01] [P19] [P22]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P19, chx:P22 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Rate and volume"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The ten-word fox sentence of the chapter lasted 6.46 s at rate 50, 5.44 s at 100, 3.62 s at 150, 2.70 s at 200 (the default), 2.16 s at 250, 1.76 s at 300 and 1.26 s at 400 when each rate was set in a fresh process (about 222 words per minute measured at the default of 200); the peak sample was 26394 at volume 1.0, 19622 at 0.75, 13024 at 0.5, 2222 at 0.1 and 0 at 0.0, volume 5 clipped to 32768, and setting the rate before each of the seven files in one process gave the same seven lengths in the recorded run [P01] [P18] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P18, chx:P19 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The WAV file"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The file save_to_file wrote for 'Hello. How are you doing?' had 1 channel, 2 bytes per sample, 22,050 Hz and 33,948 frames (1.54 s, 67,940 bytes) and was written with the wave module; hello.mp3 and hello.aiff began with RIFF and WAVE, ffprobe called the mp3 file wav, and 1,800 words of Alice's Adventures in Wonderland became 450.2 s (7.5 minutes) of audio in about 3 s of work against the chapter's 10 minutes in about 2 s [P01] [P08] [P19] [P57]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P08, chx:P19, chx:P57 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The chapter replayed"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's 17 listings were parsed from the page and replayed statement by statement (45 statements, 46 with the one that was substituted): with Python 3.14.6 and the newest packages 34 of the 46 matched the book's output, 42 ran without an exception, 1 raised and 3 were not executed (the two downloads from YouTube and the lyrics); the pinned set gave 35, 42, 1 and 3, the empty environment 8, 8, 35 and 3, and the set without ffmpeg 32, 38, 5 and 3; the statements that differ are the Voice objects and names, the Audio saved to line the driver prints, the transcription of hello.wav, the TSV listing and the call of speak [P01] [P05]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The model files"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The files of the models had 75,572,083 bytes for tiny, 145,262,807 for base, 483,617,219 for small, 1,528,008,539 for medium and 3,087,371,615 for large-v3, where Table 24-1 says 74MB, 142MB, 472MB, 1.5GB and 3GB; the package knows 14 model names including turbo (1,617,941,637 bytes), whose README table lists about 6 GB of memory against about 10 GB for large, and the first load of tiny, base and small took 0.4, 0.8 and 7.4 s (with the download for small) [P01] [P29] [P68]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P29, chx:P68 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The runtimes"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On two CPU cores the 10-word sample took 1.0 s with tiny, 1.6 s with base and 5.1 s with small, where Table 24-1 says 1.9, 3.0 and 9.6 s; the 771.6-s sample (about 13 minutes of eSpeak speech at rate 120, 1,800 words) took 40.0, 98.9 and 197.5 s where the table says 1m 20s, 2m 34s and 6m 37s for 15 minutes, with a peak resident memory of 972, 1,082 and 2,645 MiB (single runs on one machine, not the author's laptop) [P01] [P29]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P29 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "What did not run"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On the machine with 8,029 MiB of memory loading medium and turbo ended with the kernel's out-of-memory killer (return code -9), large-v3 (a 3,087,371,615-byte file) could not be loaded under a 6.5 GB address-space limit (RuntimeError, can't allocate memory), and device='cuda' raised RuntimeError because torch.cuda.is_available() is False: no GPU was used and none of the models above small was transcribed with [P01] [P29] [P68]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P29, chx:P68 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Accuracy"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The word error rate on the 10-word sentence was 0.3 for tiny, 0.2 for base and 0.1 for small, and on the 1,800 words read by eSpeak NG 0.1919, 0.1079 and 0.0345; on four LibriVox readings of the Gettysburg Address against the Wikipedia copy of the Bliss text it lay between 0.1636 and 0.3123 for tiny, 0.1524 and 0.2565 for base and 0.0186 and 0.2416 for small; ten seconds of digital silence were transcribed by tiny as ' You' and ten seconds of seeded noise as nothing [P33] [P50] [P56] [P58]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P33, chx:P50, chx:P56, chx:P58 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Languages and text"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Whisper 20250625 knows 100 languages and raised ValueError (Unsupported language: klingon) for 'Klingon'; given eSpeak NG speech in six languages tiny named the language right for 3 and base for 4, hearing the Turkish sentence as Thai both times; tiny and base wrote ' Hello, how are you doing?' for the sentence the book prints as 'Hello. How are you doing?', and a transcript is a model's text, not a copy of what was said [P01] [P29] [P33]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P29, chx:P33 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "ffmpeg"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter names ffmpeg only inside a comment of the yt-dlp listing, and Appendix A never names it, yet whisper.load_audio started ffmpeg for every file: without ffmpeg on the PATH it raised FileNotFoundError, and with it a WAV file became a float32 array of 43,129 samples at 16,000 Hz; yt-dlp without ffmpeg raised DownloadError (ffprobe and ffmpeg not found) and left downloaded_content.mp3 instead of the m4a that listing 13 looks for [P01] [P05] [P32] [P38]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05, chx:P32, chx:P38 .
chx:F19 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The subtitle writers"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For the two segments the book prints the SRT writer wrote 223 bytes, the WebVTT writer 215 and the TSV writer 190, and the SRT and VTT files equal the chapter's listings 7 and 8 (also under the pinned release); the TSV writer separates the columns with tabs and counts in milliseconds where the chapter's listing 9 shows spaces; get_writer raised KeyError for docx, the options max_line_width and word_timestamps changed a 26-block subtitle into 31 blocks and gave 14 words with times, and 52 of 52 segment times were multiples of 0.02 s [P01] [P31] [P46] [P48] [P49]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P31, chx:P46, chx:P48, chx:P49 .
chx:F20 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "yt-dlp"@en ; sen0414:findingText "yt-dlp 2026.8.19 returned a dictionary of 76 keys for the chapter's video (title Beyond the Basic Stuff with Python - Al Sweigart - Part 1, duration 565 s, 16 formats) with no video downloaded, the 13 keys the chapter prints were all present in the order of the book, and YoutubeDL has sanitize_info and no sanitize, which the prose names; on the same day one request for the same metadata failed with Sign in to confirm you're not a bot and a later one succeeded [P01] [P41] [P42]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P41, chx:P42 .
chx:F21 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Downloads"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Instead of YouTube, whose Terms of Service do not allow downloading any part of the Service or its Content except as expressly authorized, the study downloaded public-domain LibriVox readings from the Internet Archive: ydl.download returned 0 and left downloaded_content.m4a, an AAC stream of 100.3 s converted from a 64 kbit/s mp3 of 100.4 s, a string in place of a list was accepted, and an address that is nowhere raised DownloadError with HTTP Error 404 [P41] [P43] [P45] [P55] [P56]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P41, chx:P43, chx:P45, chx:P55, chx:P56 .
chx:F22 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Releases of the downloaders"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Of the last stable yt-dlp releases of 2021 to 2026, 5 of 6 ran on Python 3.14.6 and 2021.12.27 stopped with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'imghdr', a module Python 3.13 removed; of the 22 releases of pyttsx3, 8 ran, 9 (2.71 to 2.95) crashed with a segmentation fault and 5 could not be built here because they require macOS; of the 13 of openai-whisper, 6 ran on Python 3.12.3 [P14] [P15] [P16] [P28] [P40]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P14, chx:P15, chx:P16, chx:P28, chx:P40 .
chx:F23 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Interpreters"@en ; sen0414:findingText "One program that saves speech, lists voices and asks yt-dlp questions ran under 11 interpreters (3.11, 3.12, 3.13, 3.14.0 to 3.14.6 and 3.15.0b4) and compared 14 items: only 2 differed, both about aifc, audioop, sunau, chunk and sndhdr, which importing succeeded for under 3.11.17 and 3.12.3 and failed for under 3.13.14 and later (PEP 594), while wave, which pyttsx3 uses, stayed; the Whisper version of the program compared 16 items and 2 differed, only in the printed form of a type [P14] [P15]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P14, chx:P15 .
chx:F24 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The practice programs"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's listing 16 calls speak(), which no listing defines, and raised NameError in every row; a guessing game with its own speak() said 4 lines in 3 guesses and wrote WAV files of 2.56, 1.94, 1.95 and 2.5 s; the 99 bottles of beer made 99 verses, 396 lines, 2,377 words and 11,457 characters, 699.3 s (11.7 minutes) and 30.8 MB of audio in 7.5 s, and tiny heard 3 verses with a word error rate of 0.9589; the transcriber loop took 47.8 s for two archive.org readings and an address that cannot be downloaded stopped the whole list [P01] [P54]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P54 .
chx:F25 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The answers of Appendix B"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Appendix B gives 7 answers for chapter 24: answer 3 says pyttsx3 and Whisper need no online service or internet access, which held in a network namespace with no interface once the tiny model was on disk (exit code 0), while with no model file and no route loading raised URLError and with the internet 75,572,083 bytes were downloaded; answer 5 says Whisper's default model is 'base', but load_model has no default for name and the whisper command defaults to 'turbo' in 20250625 and to 'small' in 20231117 [P06] [P29]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P06, chx:P29 .
chx:F26 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Other engines and platforms"@en ; sen0414:findingText "pyttsx3 uses the SAPI 5 driver on Windows and the NSSpeechSynthesizer driver on macOS and 2.99 adds an AVSpeech driver; their sources and Microsoft's and Apple's pages were read and answered 200, but they were NOT EXECUTED; the same holds for the OpenAI, Google Cloud and Azure speech services, which need keys, and for faster-whisper, whisper.cpp, SpeechRecognition, gTTS and Vosk, whose PyPI or GitHub pages were read and which were not run [P20] [P21] [P26] [P27] [P59] [P60] [P61] [P62] [P63] [P64] [P65] [P66]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P26, chx:P27, chx:P59, chx:P60, chx:P61, chx:P62, chx:P63, chx:P64, chx:P65, chx:P66 .
chx:F27 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Limits of the study"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Nothing was heard because the machine has no sound card, no GPU and no microphone; the engines that need cloud keys, Windows, macOS and any download from YouTube were NOT EXECUTED; every timing and count above is a single run of 2026-10-14 on a machine with two cores and 8,029 MiB of memory (Ubuntu 24.04, Python 3.14.6, pyttsx3 2.99, eSpeak NG 1.51, openai-whisper 20250625, yt-dlp 2026.8.19 and, for the pins, Python 3.12.3 with pyttsx3 2.98 and openai-whisper 20231117), and the photograph was not fetched because Wikimedia answered 429 [P01] [P36] [P45] [P67] [P69]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P36, chx:P45, chx:P67, chx:P69 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "58 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "69 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-14T20:00:00; 50 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
